# The problem with uncertainty of Theoretical Foundations of Buffer Stock Saving (subsection "Model with
# Uncertainty"), under its natural borrowing constraint: the paper's own model, with its zero-income event.
# Every line of this file but its comments is the stage file of the family bst-natural/1 of llorracc/wg1-buffer-stock
# at 3901bc69 (schemas/bst/natural/bellman/natural.bl), so that a comparator reading this file reads its family's own
# stage, with the same ids and the same free parameters; only the comments are written for the paper. The stage is a
# family at least as wide as the paper's model, and the paper's model is its calibration in
# declaration/calibration/baseline.yml.
#
# The shocks' laws are bounded, with strictly positive lower and finite upper bounds, as the paper's assumption on the
# shocks requires, and the unemployment event, with probability ℘ = p_zero > 0, pays nothing. The worst income is that
# event, a point mass, so h_min, the minimum present value of future income, is 0, and the natural borrowing
# constraint is a > 0. No consumer ever ends a period at it: zero income next period would leave no positive
# consumption. So the Euler equation holds with equality and needs no complementarity clause. A positive income in
# the unemployment event, the appendix "Positive Minimum Income", needs a point mass in ψ at its floor and has no
# stage file yet (bellman/README.md). ξ is transitory income, drawn from a mixture: nothing with the event's
# probability, else the employed draw θ scaled by 1/(1 − ℘), so that E[ξ] = 1. θ's law is stated by its own
# parameters and ξ's employed component is derived from them, the same law: θ·scale, θ truncated lognormal, is
# truncated lognormal with location μ_θ + log scale and bounds θ_min·scale and θ_max·scale. ξ is drawn whole, and not
# as θ and an event's label with a line defining ξ, so that what a toolkit holds, the nodes of ξ, is what the stage
# draws. ϱ > 1, the paper's coefficient of relative risk aversion ρ, which this family requires to exceed one.
# Survivors only: newborns, types and their shares, and the stationary population have no spelling in a stage file.
#
# The paper writes Γ for the growth factor, ρ for relative risk aversion and ℘ for the probability of zero income; the
# stage writes 𝒢, ϱ and p_zero, the comparator's names, which its methods files and its checks read.

@stage: natural

Xm := ℝ₊₊
Xa := ℝ₊₊

β     : ℝ₊₊          :: ["discount factor of the type"]
ℒ     : (0,1]        :: ["probability of surviving into the next period, perpetual youth"]
ϱ     : (1, inf)     :: ["coefficient of relative risk aversion, greater than one as in Buffer Stock Theory"]
R     : ℝ₊₊          :: ["gross return on assets, earned by survivors, with no annuity market"]
𝒢     : ℝ₊₊          :: ["growth factor of permanent income"]
μ_ψ   : ℝ            :: ["location of log ψ before truncation, set so that E[ψ] = 1"]
s_ψ   : ℝ₊₊          :: ["scale of log ψ before truncation"]
# ψ's floor is positive and otherwise free. The constraint is the natural one, −h_min, minus the present value of the
# least future income; here the least income is zero, the unemployment event's, so h_min = 0 at any growth of
# permanent income: the present value of nothing. That growth at ψ's floor lie below the return, 𝒢*ψ_min < R, is the
# condition, where the least income is positive, for its present value to stay bounded as the horizon recedes; it is
# not required here, and the paper's model has its solution without it.
ψ_min  : ℝ₊₊          :: ["the lower bound of ψ, strictly positive"]
ψ_max  : ℝ₊₊          :: ["the upper bound of ψ, finite"]
μ_θ   : ℝ            :: ["location of log θ before truncation, set so that E[θ] = 1"]
s_θ   : ℝ₊₊          :: ["scale of log θ before truncation"]
θ_min  : ℝ₊₊          :: ["the lower bound of θ, strictly positive"]
θ_max  : ℝ₊₊          :: ["the upper bound of θ, finite"]
p_zero : (0,1)       :: ["probability ℘ of the zero-income event, positive"]

ξ_scale   : ℝ₊₊ := 1/(1 - p_zero)     :: ["the employed draw's scale in ξ, 1/(1 − ℘), so that E[ξ] = 1"]
μ_ξ       : ℝ   := μ_θ + log(ξ_scale)   :: ["location of log ξ's employed component before truncation"]
ξ_emp_min : ℝ₊₊ := θ_min*ξ_scale      :: ["the lower bound of ξ's employed component"]
ξ_emp_max : ℝ₊₊ := θ_max*ξ_scale      :: ["the upper bound of ξ's employed component"]

ψ : ℝ₊₊
ψ ~ TruncatedLogNormal(μ_ψ, s_ψ, ψ_min, ψ_max)   :: ["permanent shock, E[ψ] = 1"]
ξ : ℝ₊
ξ ~ Mixture((p_zero, 0), (1 - p_zero, TruncatedLogNormal(μ_ξ, s_θ, ξ_emp_min, ξ_emp_max)))   :: ["transitory income, E[ξ] = 1: zero when unemployed, with probability p_zero, else θ/(1 − ℘); independent of ψ"]

𝑢(c : ℝ₊₊) := c^(1-ϱ)/(1-ϱ)       :: [id=util]
𝑑𝑢(c : ℝ₊₊) := c^(-ϱ)             :: [id=marg_util]
𝑑𝑢𝑖𝑛𝑣(x : ℝ₊₊) := x^(-1/ϱ)        :: [id=inv_marg_util]
𝒞(x : Xm) := (0, x)   :: [id=feasible, "consumption strictly below resources: a > 0"]

[id=arvl_to_dcsn, (k : Xa, ψ, ξ) : !arvl -> (m : Xm) : !dcsn] {
    m = R*k/(𝒢*ψ) + ξ                                   :: [id=market_resources, "a survivor's resources; k is last period's a"]
}

[id=dcsn_to_cntn, (m, c : ℝ₊₊ @ !ctrl) : !dcsn -> (a : Xa) : !cntn] {
    a = m - c                         :: [id=savings]
}

[id=cntn_to_dcsn, (V[>] : ℝ @ !vlu, c) : !cntn -> (V : ℝ @ !vlu) : !dcsn] {
    V      = 𝑢(c) + β*ℒ*V[>]          :: [id=value]
    D_{m}V = 𝑑𝑢(c)                    :: [id=marginal]
}

[id=dcsn_to_arvl, (V) : !dcsn -> (V[<] : ℝ @ !vlu) : !arvl] {
    V[<]      = E_{ψ,ξ}((𝒢*ψ)^(1-ϱ) * V)               :: [id=value]
    D_{k}V[<] = E_{ψ,ξ}(R * (𝒢*ψ)^(-ϱ) * D_{m}V)       :: [id=marginal]
}

[id=policy, (V[>] : ℝ @ !vlu) : !cntn -> (c : ℝ₊₊ @ !ctrl) : !dcsn] {
    c     = argmax_{cp : 𝒞(m)}(𝑢(cp) + β*ℒ*V[>])   :: [id=optimality, !doc]
    𝑑𝑢(c) = β*ℒ*D_{a}V[>]   :: [id=euler, "with equality: the natural constraint is never reached"]
}
